readings.c1s1:
{{smaller block|''Fal.'' For their bareness, I am sure they never learned that of me.
''Prince.'' . . . Unless you call three fingers on the ribs ''bare.''
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., iv. 2.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Bare contract''', in ''law'', an unconditional promise or surrender.—'''Bare wind†''', ''naut.'', a wind that is scant, or too much ahead to fill the sails.—'''The bare.''' (''a'') In ''art'', the nude. [Rare.] (''b''†) The uncovered or unhidden surface; the body; the substance. [Rare.]}}
{{smaller block|You have touched ''the'' very ''bare'' of truth.
{{right|''Marston.''}}}}
{{smaller|'''To lay bare''', to uncover; expose to view or to knowledge, as something hidden or a secret of any kind.—'''Under bare poles''' (''naut.''), said of a ship with no sail set, in a gale of wind. ='''Syn.''' See ''mere''.}}

'''bare'''<sup>1</sup> (bãr), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''bared'', ppr. ''baring''. [< ME. ''baren'', < AS. ''barian'' (in comp. ''ābarian''), also ''berian'' (= OHG. ''barōn'' = Icel. ''bera''), make bare, < ''bær'', bare: see ''bare''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.''] '''1.''' To make bare; uncover; divest of covering: as, to ''bare'' one’s head or one’s breast.
{{smaller block|He ''bared'' an ancient oak of all her boughs.
{{right|''Dryden.''}}
<poem>That cry . . . that seemed to ''bare''
A wretched life of every softening veil.</poem>
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, II. 159.}}}}
'''2.''' To disclose; make manifest; lay bare: as, to ''bare'' the secrets of the grave. [Archaic.]

'''bare'''<sup>2</sup> (bãr). Old preterit of ''bear''<sup>1</sup>.

'''Barea''' (bā′rē-ä̤), ''n. pl.'' [Gr., neut. pl. of βαρύς, βαρέα, heavy.] An Aristotelian group of birds, corresponding to the Linnean ''Gallinæ'', including the gallinaceous or rasorial birds.

'''bareback''' (bãr′bak), ''a.'' and ''adv.'' '''I.''' ''a.'' Using or performing on a barebacked horse: as, a ''bareback'' rider.

'''II.''' ''adv.'' On a barebacked horse: as, to ride ''bareback''.

readings.c1s3:
¶
'''barefacedness''' (bãr′fāst-nes), ''n.'' '''1.''' Openness. —'''2.''' Effrontery; assurance; audaciousness.

'''barefit''' (bãr′fit), ''a.'' Barefoot or barefooted. [Scotch.]

'''barefoot''' (bãr′fu̇t), ''a.'' and ''adv.'' [< ME. ''barefote'', ''barfot'', < AS. ''bærfōt'' (= OFries. ''berfōt'' = D. ''barrevoet'' = Icel. ''berfættr''), < ''bær'', bare, + ''fōt'', foot.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Having the feet bare; without shoes and stockings.
{{smaller block|Going to find a ''barefoot'' brother out,<br>One of our order. {{right|''Shak.'', R. and J., v. 2.}}
Blessings on thee, little man,<br>''Barefoot'' boy, with cheek of tan!
{{right|''Whittier'', Barefoot Boy.}}}}

'''II.''' ''adv.'' With the feet bare.
{{smaller block|I must dance ''barefoot.'' {{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., ii. 1.}}}}

'''barefooted''' (bãr′fu̇t-ed), ''a.'' [< ''barefoot'' + ''-ed''<sup>1</sup>.] Having the feet bare.—'''Barefooted Augustinians.''' {{smaller|See ''Augustinian.''}}—'''Barefooted Carmelites.''' {{smaller|See ''Carmelite.''}}

'''barege''' (ba-räzh′), ''n.'' [< F. ''barège'', so called from ''Barèges'', a watering-place in the Pyrenees. See def.] A thin gauze-like fabric for women’s dresses, usually made of silk and worsted, but, in the inferior sorts, with cotton in place of silk.
{{smaller block|In reality bareges were never made in the village from which they have their name, the seat of the manufacture being at Bagnères-de-Bigorre in the Pyrenees.}}

'''baregin, baregine''' (ba-rā′zhin), ''n.'' [< ''Barèges'' (see ''barege''), the springs of which yield the sub-

readings.c2s1:
stance, + ''-in''<sup>2</sup>.] A transparent, gelatinous, mucus-like substance, the product of certain algæ growing in thermal sulphur-springs, to which they impart the flavor and odor of flesh-broth. {{smaller|Baregin is itself odorless and tasteless. It contains, when dry, from 30 to 80 per cent. of mineral matter, chiefly silica. The organic matter contains no sulphur and from 9 to 12 per cent. of nitrogen.}}

'''bare-gnawn†''' (bãr′nân), ''a.'' Gnawed or eaten bare. ''Shak.'', Lear, v. 3.

'''barehanded''' (bãr′han″ded), ''a.'' '''1.''' With uncovered hands.—'''2.''' Destitute of means; with no aid but one’s own hands: as, he began life ''barehanded''.

'''bareheaded''' (bãr′hed″ed), ''a.'' Having the head uncovered, especially as a token of respect.
{{smaller block|<poem>First, you shall swear never to name my lord,
Or hear him nam’d hereafter, but ''bare-headed.''</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and another''?), Queen of Corinth, iv. 1.}}
On being first brought before the court, Ridley stood ''bareheaded.'' {{right|''Froude'', Hist. Eng., xxxiii.}}}}

'''bareheadedness''' (bãr′hed″ed-nes), ''n.'' The state of being bareheaded.
{{smaller block|''Bareheadedness'' was in Corinth, as also in all Greece and Rome, a token of honour and superiority.
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Remains, p. 237.}}}}

'''barely''' (bãr′li), ''adv.'' [< ''bare''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1.''' Nakedly; openly; without disguise or concealment.—'''2.''' Scantily; poorly: as, a man ''barely'' clad, or a room ''barely'' furnished.—'''3.''' Only just; no more than; with nothing over or to spare: as, she is ''barely'' sixteen.
{{smaller block|In paying his debts a man ''barely'' does his duty.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', The Bee, No. 3.}}
Fox himself ''barely'' succeeded in retaining his seat for}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|Westminster.
{{right|''Lecky'', Eng. in 18th Cent., xv.}}}}

'''4.''' Merely; only. [Archaic.]

{{smaller block|It is not ''barely'' a man’s abridgment in his external accommodations which makes him miserable.
{{right|''South.''}}}}

'''bareman†''' (bãr′man), ''n.'' [Sc., also ''bairman''; < ''bare''<sup>1</sup> + ''man.''] A bankrupt. [Scotch.]

'''bareness''' (bãr′nes), ''n.'' The state of being bare. {{smaller|(''a'') Want or deficiency of clothing or covering; nakedness. (''b'') Deficiency of appropriate covering, equipment, furniture, ornament, etc.: as, “old December’s ''bareness,''” ''Shak.'', Sonnets, xcvii.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>To make old ''bareness'' picturesque,
And tuft with grass a feudal tower.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', In Memoriam, cxxviii.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') Leanness. [Rare.] (''d'') Poverty; indigence.}}

{{smaller block|Stript of . . . its Priveleges, and made like the primitive Church for its ''Bareness.''
{{right|''South'', Sermons, I. 229.}}}}

'''bare-picked''' (bãr′pikt), ''a.'' Picked bare; stripped of all flesh, as a bone.

{{smaller block|The ''bare-picked'' bone of majesty.
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, iv. 3.}}}}

'''bare-pump''' (bãr′pump), ''n.'' A pump for drawing liquor from a cask: used in vinegar-works, wine- and beer-cellars, in sampling, etc. Also called ''bar-pump.''

'''bare-ribbed''' (bãr′ribd), ''a.'' With bare ribs like a skeleton: as, “''bare-ribbed'' death,” ''Shak.'', K. John, v. 2.

'''bares''', ''n.'' Plural of ''baris'', 1.

'''baresark''' (bãr′särk), ''n.'' [< ''bare''<sup>1</sup> + ''sark''; a lit. translation of ''berserker'', Icel. ''berserkr'', in the supposed sense of ‘bare shirt’; but see ''berserker.''] A berserk or berserker.

{{smaller block|Many of Harold’s brothers in arms fell, and on his own ship every man before the mast, except his band of ''Baresarks'', was either wounded or slain.
{{right|''Edinburgh Rev.''}}}}

readings.c3s1:
ganha = It. ''bargagna'' (Pr. also ''barganh'' = It. ''bargagno''), < ML. *''barcania'', *''barcanium'', a bargain, traffic; cf. ''bargain'', ''v.'' Origin unknown; supposed by Diez and others to be from ML. ''barca'', a boat, bark, or barge, but evidence is wanting.] '''1†.''' The act of discussing the terms of a proposed agreement; bargaining.
{{smaller block|<poem>I’ll give thrice so much land
To any well-deserving friend;
But in the way of ''bargain'', mark ye me,
I’ll cavil on the ninth part of a hair.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., iii. 1.}}}}
'''2†.''' A contention or contest for the mastery or upper hand; a struggle.
{{smaller block|On Brudus side the better of that bloudie ''bargaine'' went.
{{right|''Warner'', Albion’s Eng., XIV. xc. 365. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''3.''' A contract or an agreement between two or more parties; a compact settling that something shall be done; specifically, a contract by which one party binds himself to transfer the right to some property for a consideration, and the other party binds himself to receive the property and pay the consideration.
{{smaller block|<poem>To clap this royal ''bargain'' up of peace.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, iii. 1.}}
<poem>''Ros.'' But if you do refuse to marry me,
You’ll give yourself to this most faithful shepherd?
''Phe.'' So is the ''bargain''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, v. 4.}}
“Our fathers,” said one orator, “sold their king for southern gold, and we still lie under the reproach of that foul ''bargain''.”
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., vi.}}}}
'''4.''' The outcome of an agreement as regards

readings.c3s2:
one of the parties; that which is acquired by bargaining; the thing purchased or stipulated for: as, look at my ''bargain''; a bad ''bargain''; “a losing ''bargain,''” ''Junius'', Letters, v.
{{smaller block|She was too fond of her most filthy ''bargain.''
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, v. 2.}}}}
'''5.''' Something bought or sold at a low price; an advantageous purchase.
{{smaller block|If you have a taste for paintings, egad, you shall have ’em a ''bargain.'' {{right|''Sheridan'', School for Scandal, iii. 3.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Bargain and sale''', or, more fully, ''deed of bargain and sale'', in ''law'', the form of deed now in common use for the conveyance of land: so called because it is expressed as a sale for a pecuniary consideration agreed on, being thus distinguished on the one hand from a quitclaim, which is a release, and on the other hand from the old conveyance by covenant to stand seized to uses.—'''Dutch''' or '''wet bargain,''' a bargain sealed by the parties drinking over it.—'''Into the bargain,''' over and above what is stipulated; moreover; besides.}}
{{smaller block|Faith, Charles, this is the most convenient thing you could have found for the business, for ’twill serve not only as a hammer, but a catalogue ''into the bargain.''
{{right|''Sheridan'', School for Scandal, iv. 1.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To beat a bargain,''' to bargain; haggle.—'''To buy at a bargain,''' to buy cheaply.—'''To buy the bargain dear†,''' to pay dearly for a thing.—'''To make the best of a bad bargain,''' to do the best one can in untoward circumstances.}}
{{smaller block|I am sorry for thy misfortune; however, we must ''make the best of a bad bargain.'' {{right|''Arbuthnot'', Hist. of John Bull.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To sell a bargain†,''' to entrap one into asking innocent questions, so as to give an unexpected answer, usually a coarse or indelicate one.}}
{{smaller block|The boy hath ''sold'' him ''a bargain.'' {{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., iii. 1.}}
<poem>I see him ogle still, and hear him chat;
''Selling'' facetious ''bargains'', and propounding
That witty recreation call’d dumfounding.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Prol. to Prophetess, l. 46.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>No maid at court is less asham’d,
Howe’er for ''selling bargains'' fam’d. {{right|''Swift.''}}</poem>}}

'''To strike a bargain''', {{smaller|to complete or ratify a bargain or an agreement, originally by ''striking'' or shaking hands.}} ='''Syn. 3.''' {{smaller|Covenant, mutual engagement.}}

'''bargain''' (bär′gān), ''v.'' [< ME. ''bargainen'', ''bargaynen'', etc., < OF. ''bargaigner'' (F. ''barguigner'') = Pr. Pg. ''barganhar'' = It. ''bargagnare'', < ML. ''barcaniare'', traffic, trade, < *''barcania'', traffic: see the noun.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To treat about a transaction; make terms.
{{smaller block|The thrifty state will ''bargain'' ere they fight. {{right|''Dryden.''}}}}
'''2.''' To come to or make an agreement; stipulate; make or strike a bargain: ''with'' a person, ''for'' an object: as, he ''bargained with'' the producers ''for'' a daily supply.
{{smaller block|<poem>So worthless peasants ''bargain for'' their wives
As market-men ''for'' oxen, sheep, or horse.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., v. 5.}}</poem>
I alighted, and having ''bargained with'' my host ''for'' 20 crownes a moneth, I caused a good fire to be made in my chamber. {{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Nov. 4, 1644.}}}}
'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To arrange beforehand by negotiation and agreement.
{{smaller block|<poem>’Tis ''bargain’d'' . . .
That she shall still be curst in company.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., ii. 1.}}</poem>}}
'''2†.''' To agree to buy or sell.—'''To bargain away''', {{smaller|to part with or lose as the result of a bargain.}}
{{smaller block|The heir . . . had somehow ''bargained away'' the estate. {{right|''George Eliot'', Felix Holt, Int.}}}}

'''bargain-chop''' (bär′gān-chop), ''n.'' A kind of gambling “option” on opium to arrive, formerly common among foreign traders in China.
